course: dictionary
lesson: 5730
title: Lorry and truck
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A lorry is a big vehicle that carries goods along roads. Goods are things that are bought, sold or moved, such as food, boxes or furniture. «The lorry brought food to the shop this morning» tells you the lorry carried goods to a shop.
explain: A lorry load is the amount one lorry carries in one trip. Look at the words around a word to find its meaning. Here «lorry» comes before «load», so a lorry load must be what fits on one lorry.
explain: In America and Canada, people say truck instead of lorry. If you read «a truck pulled a heavy trailer», you know it is the same kind of big vehicle that a British reader would call a lorry.
explain: Staying safe near lorries matters. «You never ride up the inside of a lorry or truck at a junction, because the driver cannot see you.» Keep well back from a lorry, and never walk or cycle right beside one.
fact: A lorry is a big vehicle for carrying goods on roads.
fact: In America and Canada, a lorry is called a truck.
fact: A lorry load is the amount one lorry carries.
fact: «A full lorry can weigh as much as thirty cars.»
fact: A lorry driver cannot see right next to the lorry, so never walk or cycle close beside one.
exercise: «The lorry brought food to the shop this morning.» What is a lorry? | A kind of ship that sails on rivers | A small bicycle for one rider | *A big vehicle for carrying goods on roads | A sort of shop for food
exercise: An American writes, «The truck was loaded with boxes.» What would a British child call that vehicle? | A tram | *A lorry | A bus | A bicycle
exercise: «A lorry load of sand was tipped on the road.» What does lorry load mean here? | The weight of a bus | A load for a big family | *The amount one lorry carries | A lorry that is empty
test: «A lighter car speeds up faster than a heavy lorry with the same engine.» Why does the lorry speed up more slowly? | *It is heavier, so the same engine has more weight to move | It is painted a brighter colour | It has no engine | It has fewer wheels
test: Ella reads the joke «Fell off the back of a lorry» and thinks the goods were given away as a present. What does the joke really mean? | The goods were lost in the rain | *The goods were stolen | The goods were sold at a market | The goods fell and were broken
exam: «The driver of a truck or lorry cannot see a cyclist in its blind spots.» What should a cyclist do? | *Keep well back from a lorry, out of its blind spots | Hold on to the back of a lorry | Ride right beside a lorry so the driver can see them | Wave at the driver from behind the lorry
